A||||5||Bazooka||Cast Iron Integrity||||1||
Z||000000||Bazooka||10-14-2001||03:17 PM||||I have a question for anyone that has experience with this or may know about metallurgy or foundry/casting. I am thinking of removing some threaded bosses on a cast iron exhaust manifold for clearance reasons. The exhaust manifolds are 2 1/2" outlets and would fit nicely under my V8. Would I be compromising the strength or integrity of the manifold by cutting off the bosses and grinding the area smooth as shown in picture below? TIA<BR>*********************************<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.members.aol.com:/m34chev/em25.jpg">     <P>*********************************<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-14-2001||03:23 PM||||As long as you saw it off it shouldn't be a problem. With cast there are never any guaratees.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||10-14-2001||03:29 PM||||Yeah, don't use heat. However, have fun trying to CUT it off... Cast iron is HARD. A die grinder with a tungsten cutter <I>should</I> do it. Just be careful not to let it heat up too much. Cast iron is Granular, so I don't think you would be compromising it too much.||||reg||10
Z||000003||sluppy123||10-14-2001||07:43 PM||||I see no problem in removing the boss, but remember that cast is just that-"cast" so there could be a air pocket in there. I'm not sure of the quality of the casting, but it looks like a good one, all sharp corners are sharp.<P>The cast iron used in manifolds shouldn't be all that hard, a hack saw and a die grinder should do it. The best way to remove it would be to have a machinist mill it off for you.<P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>                <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=54316496&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||10-14-2001||09:44 PM||||Actually cast iron is soft. Don't use heat to cut that boss off, use a cutoff saw. Grind any sharp edges smooth to prevent cracks from forming and you should be fine. It looks like once you cut off that boss it will be just like the opposite side where there is no boss.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bazooka||10-14-2001||11:15 PM||||*********************************<BR>Thanks Ogre, Mach, Sluppy, and Formula for the useful suggestions regarding the treatment and machining for the manifolds.<BR> <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <P>***********************************<P>||||reg||1
